      "body": "After breaking through Thermopylae, Persian forces entered Attica while Athenians evacuated their city. Allied Greek ships assembled around Salamis, where Themistocles advocated fighting in confined waters. Their victory weakened naval support for Xerxes’ expedition. The Persian king withdrew to Asia, but left a substantial land army in Greece: the campaign did not end with the naval battle.",
      "context": "The invasion grew from conflicts between the Persian Empire and Greek communities around the Aegean. The Greek world was politically divided. Some cities resisted Persia, while others submitted or remained outside the coalition, making allied cooperation a continuing political achievement.",
      "outcome": "Victories at Plataea and Mycale in the following year remained essential. Athens gained naval prestige and experience in mobilizing its citizens, helping to underpin the later Delian League and Athenian empire. The battle should not be reduced to a predetermined contest between civilizations.",
      "locationNote": "The marker represents Salamis Island; fighting took place in waters between the island and Attica, not at one exact point.",
      "sortOrder": 0
