      "title": "Alexander’s death and the succession struggles",
      "summary": "Alexander’s death at Babylon left no secure succession, and rival commanders gradually divided his empire.",
      "body": "After returning from his campaigns, Alexander fell ill and died in Babylon. Ancient accounts and modern calendar reconstructions differ over the precise day and cause; poisoning cannot be presented as established fact. With an immature or unborn next generation of heirs and a powerful army, commanders initially allocated responsibilities under a shared monarchy. Competition over regency, territory, and military resources then produced decades of war.",
