      "summary": "Three wars between Rome and Carthage transformed Roman power from an Italian alliance into a Mediterranean empire.",
      "body": "The First Punic War centered on Sicily, where Rome built a navy and gained the advantage. In the second, Hannibal crossed from Iberia through the Alps and inflicted major defeats in Italy. Rome nevertheless retained sufficient allies and manpower to continue and carried the war into Africa. The third conflict took place under a profound imbalance of power: Rome besieged and destroyed Carthage in 146 BCE.",
      "context": "Carthaginian commerce, naval strength, and North African resources encountered an expanding Roman alliance system. The three wars differed in causes and military balance. They resulted from successive regional crises, rather than one predetermined century-long plan.",
      "outcome": "Rome emerged as the dominant western Mediterranean power, gaining provinces, revenue, land, and enslaved captives while intensifying military demands and inequalities of wealth. Carthage’s destruction and violence against its inhabitants were part of expansion. Rome also acquired the political burden of governing overseas societies with diverse institutions and traditions.",
