      "summary": "Hannibal encircled and devastated a Roman army in southern Italy, but the tactical victory did not force Rome to surrender.",
      "body": "Rome concentrated a large army to confront Hannibal. Near Cannae, the Carthaginian commander allowed his center to absorb the Roman advance while troops on the flanks and cavalry closed around it. Roman losses were severe, and some Italian allies changed sides. Nevertheless, Rome continued recruiting and maintaining its alliances, increasingly avoiding another engagement on the same terms.",
      "context": "Hannibal was campaigning far from his North African base and needed to break Rome’s Italian alliance network. Rome’s substantial manpower and resources depended on preserving that network. Battlefield success could end the war only if it changed those political relationships.",
      "outcome": "Cannae became a major example in the study of encirclement tactics. Ancient troop and casualty totals differ and should not be treated as exact measurements. Roman resistance prolonged the war until campaigning shifted to Africa, showing why a battle’s outcome cannot simply stand for the outcome of a war.",
