      "date": "August 410 CE",
      "title": "Alaric’s forces sack Rome",
      "summary": "Gothic troops entered and plundered Rome, shocking imperial society without ending western imperial government that year.",
      "body": "Alaric’s army sought supplies, settlement, and an official imperial position while alternately negotiating and fighting with the western court. The execution of Stilicho deepened the crisis, and Rome endured sieges. In August 410, Gothic forces entered and plundered the city for several days, exposing inhabitants to loss and violence. Honorius continued to govern from Ravenna: the sack of Rome and the overthrow of imperial government were distinct events.",
      "context": "Goths and Romans were not permanently separated into simple categories of outsiders and defenders. Many Goths had served in imperial armies or formed alliances with emperors. Disputes over payment, settlement, and command turned cooperation into war amid internal Roman rivalries.",
      "outcome": "The symbolic blow provoked arguments among Christians and adherents of traditional religion about responsibility and history. Augustine’s City of God belonged to this intellectual response. Western imperial rule survived for decades, and Rome was not completely destroyed; the sack belongs within a longer political transformation.",
      "sortOrder": 800
