      "title": "Clovis accepts Catholic baptism",
      "summary": "The Frankish king’s baptism strengthened ties with Gallic bishops, although its exact year remains disputed.",
      "body": "Clovis expanded Frankish power through war and alliance and received baptism from Remigius of Reims. Gregory of Tours linked his conversion to a battlefield vow and the influence of Queen Clotild. Later tradition commonly commemorates 496, but other evidence and modern interpretations support a later date, possibly 508. The conventional anniversary should therefore not be treated as certain. His choice aligned the king with the faith of many Gallo-Roman elites.",
      "context": "After western Roman political fragmentation, bishops, landed aristocrats, and military rulers jointly sustained local authority. Other Germanic kingdoms included rulers of Arian or related Christian traditions. Religious differences intersected with political alliances rather than explaining every conflict by themselves.",
      "outcome": "Ties between Clovis and the bishops helped consolidate Merovingian monarchy and became an important later royal memory. Baptism did not create modern France immediately, nor did all Franks convert at once. Christianization across local communities continued over a much longer period.",
      "sortOrder": 0
