      "context": "North Africa had entered the political networks of Islamic expansion, and people and trade already crossed the strait. Visigothic succession disputes and divided local power weakened coordinated resistance. Local elites fought, negotiated or surrendered, creating varied experiences of the new rule.",
      "outcome": "New political centres and social relationships developed, with Córdoba later becoming a major capital. Muslim, Christian and Jewish communities experienced both exchange and unequal legal conditions. Independent polities survived in the north, and frontiers continued to change rather than settling permanently in 711.",
