      "date": "732 CE",
      "title": "The Battle of Tours–Poitiers",
      "summary": "Charles Martel defeated an Andalusi army and strengthened his political position; the precise battlefield and scale remain uncertain.",
      "body": "Abd al-Rahman, governor of al-Andalus, led forces across the Pyrenees through Aquitaine and northward. Duke Eudes sought assistance from Charles Martel, and the opposing armies met somewhere between Tours and Poitiers. Abd al-Rahman was killed and his army withdrew. Later narratives offer more detail than contemporary evidence can securely establish about troop numbers, tactics, or terrain.",
      "context": "Frankish power brokers, rulers in Aquitaine, and Muslim authorities in Iberia competed across a shifting frontier. Cross-border expeditions combined plunder, political pressure, and expansion. Their objectives cannot simply be inferred from later religious narratives.",
      "outcome": "The victory increased Charles’s influence in the Frankish world and Aquitaine, but did not immediately end Muslim activity north of the Pyrenees. Calling it a single battle that inevitably “saved Europe” obscures the importance of North African rebellion, Iberian politics, and prolonged frontier competition.",
