      "summary": "Northern raiders attacked Lindisfarne’s monastery, prompting widespread religious reflection and becoming a famous marker of expanding Viking activity.",
      "body": "Attackers arriving from the North Sea plundered Lindisfarne and inflicted violence on its religious community. As a major centre of St Cuthbert’s cult with links across England and the continent, the monastery’s suffering drew a wide response. Alcuin and other writers interpreted the disaster through sin and divine judgment, expressing grief and urging changes in conduct. Their letters preserve a vivid record of early raids entering public discussion within Latin Christianity.",
