      "date": "863 CE",
      "title": "Cyril and Methodius’s Slavonic mission",
      "summary": "The Byzantine brothers developed Slavonic worship and translation in Great Moravia, helping establish a lasting written tradition.",
      "body": "After Rostislav of Great Moravia requested missionaries from Constantinople, Emperor Michael III and Patriarch Photius chose Cyril and Methodius. They began their work around 863, using Slavonic for worship and translating religious texts. Their mission is associated with the creation of the Glagolitic alphabet. Clergy asserting Latin liturgical practice and jurisdiction opposed them, and the brothers traveled to Rome for recognition. Disciples continued the work.",
      "context": "Central European rulers sought room for maneuver among Byzantium, the papacy, and East Frankish church authorities. Liturgical language concerned understanding as well as episcopal jurisdiction, political independence, and the training of local clergy.",
      "outcome": "The translations and the work of their successors profoundly influenced Slavic Christian literature. Their transmission involved expulsion, migration, and reorganization in different regions. Modern Cyrillic should not be described as an alphabet invented in its finished form by the brothers in 863; later scholars also shaped these writing traditions.",
