      "title": "Legal study and the emergence of Bologna’s university",
      "summary": "Legal teaching and student associations gradually developed into a university; 1088 is a conventional anniversary, not a securely documented founding day.",
      "body": "In the eleventh and twelfth centuries, students gathered in Bologna to study law, and teachers built influential traditions of explaining and commenting on Roman legal texts. Lessons occupied private houses and urban spaces rather than a campus created by a dated royal decree. Growing numbers of students organized mutual assistance by geographical origin. These associations later developed into corporations able to negotiate fees, teaching, and treatment by civic authorities.",
      "context": "Commerce, urban government, and church administration required people trained to interpret law and produce documents. Roman and canon law answered these needs. Students arriving from other regions also made accommodation, protection, and legal jurisdiction matters of practical negotiation.",
      "outcome": "Bologna became an influential model for European legal education and university organization. Its conventional date of 1088 supports commemoration, not proof of a complete founding ceremony in that year. The university emerged as a scholarly community and legal institution over several generations.",
