      "date": "Teaching attested by 1096",
      "title": "Early teaching and the growth of Oxford",
      "summary": "Teaching is documented at Oxford by 1096; migration, recognition, and later colleges gradually shaped the university.",
      "body": "No single document establishes an exact founding day for Oxford, but teaching existed there by 1096. Scholarly networks and movement across the Channel expanded local intellectual life. Growth accelerated after Henry II barred English students from attending Paris in 1167. Masters and students gradually organized a community and negotiated with townspeople, royal government, and church authorities. Colleges established later provided lasting support for some scholars.",
      "context": "Medieval advanced learning drew on church schools and organized knowledge through texts, lectures, and disputation. Scholars often enjoyed particular clerical or legal privileges. These attracted students but also created disputes with townspeople over rent, public order, and jurisdiction.",
      "outcome": "Oxford became an enduring center of higher education in the English-speaking world. The early university, its later colleges, and the modern research institution did not emerge simultaneously. Its development depended on migration, patronage, and negotiated autonomy rather than one monarch or one building.",
