      "summary": "Crusaders captured Jerusalem after a long expedition, massacred inhabitants, and established a center of Latin rule in the eastern Mediterranean.",
      "body": "Armies answering the call of 1095 crossed Europe and Anatolia amid hunger, fighting, and internal disputes. They besieged Jerusalem, then controlled by the Fatimid state, and breached its defenses in July 1099. The capture brought massacres of Muslim and Jewish inhabitants and extensive plunder; survivors also faced captivity and ransom. The commanders then organized government, with Godfrey taking a leading position.",
      "context": "The expedition combined pilgrimage, penitence, territorial ambition, and aristocratic politics with Byzantine requests for military assistance. The Middle East included competing Muslim governments and diverse Christian communities. The crusaders did not encounter a single, unified religious bloc.",
      "outcome": "Latin Jerusalem became part of a wider system of crusader states sustained by migration, ports, local accommodation, and military support. The conquest and massacres generated lasting hostility. Inflated casualty figures in narrative sources are not reliable population counts, and encounters between religious communities extended beyond warfare.",
