      "date": "26 August 1346",
      "title": "The Battle of Crécy",
      "summary": "Edward III’s forces defeated a French army through defensive deployment and coordination with archers, then moved toward Calais.",
      "body": "After campaigning from Normandy into northern France, Edward III was pursued by Philip VI’s army to the vicinity of Crécy. English forces deployed dismounted men-at-arms and longbowmen on favorable ground. Attacks by the French and their allies failed to maintain effective coordination. Missile fire, positioning, command decisions, and disorder all contributed to serious French losses, including the death of King John of Bohemia. One weapon alone does not explain the result.",
      "context": "Early in the Hundred Years’ War, both crowns mobilized troops through feudal obligations, contracts, and international alliances. English raiding damaged the countryside and pressured the French king to respond. The battle formed part of this wider campaign of movement and supply.",
      "outcome": "Victory enabled Edward to besiege Calais, captured in the following year and retained as a durable English base. Crécy enhanced English military prestige but did not destroy French royal power. Troop numbers, losses, and weapon performance require cautious comparison of sources rather than reliance on inflated totals.",
      "locationNote": "The marker identifies the settlement of Crécy. Fighting took place nearby; modern memorial locations do not by themselves fix the medieval battle lines.",
      "sortOrder": 826
