      "locationNote": "Messina was one of the important ports affected by the plague in Mediterranean Europe in 1347; this entry follows the epidemic’s wider spread.",
      "context": "Trade and travel connected settlements and ecological environments, while earlier food difficulties increased vulnerability in some regions. Contemporaries interpreted disease through religion and the medical ideas available to them. Existing inequalities and prejudices influenced both assistance and conflict during the emergency.",
      "outcome": "Population loss created labour shortages and gave some survivors opportunities to seek higher wages or better terms, while landowners and governments attempted to contain those changes. Jewish communities and others suffered false accusations and persecution. Recurrent outbreaks continued to reshape labour relations, charity and public-health practices across different regions.",
