      "date": "19 September 1356",
      "title": "The Battle of Poitiers and the capture of John II",
      "summary": "The Black Prince’s army defeated John II, whose capture intensified France’s financial and political crisis.",
      "body": "Edward, prince of Wales, led English, Welsh, and Gascon troops from Aquitaine on a destructive campaign into France. John II pursued them, and the armies fought near Poitiers. The prince’s forces used terrain, archers, and dismounted men-at-arms to withstand attacks before counterattacking. The capture of the king and prominent nobles quickly turned military defeat into problems of ransom, taxation, and the exercise of royal authority.",
      "context": "War had expanded again after a truce. Raiding aimed to damage enemy revenue and force a response. Military obligations were closely tied to noble status, so the king’s defeat undermined confidence in the governing elite’s ability to protect the kingdom.",
      "outcome": "Captivity intensified demands for negotiation and reform. The dauphin faced political challenges in Paris and unrest in the countryside. The Treaty of Brétigny in 1360 required a ransom and major territorial concessions, but fighting later resumed. Victory created a severe crisis without placing France permanently under English rule.",
      "locationNote": "This is a reference point for Poitiers. The battle occurred outside the city, not at the city-center marker.",
