      "date": "May–June 1358",
      "title": "The Jacquerie",
      "summary": "A rural uprising against nobles spread through northern France amid wartime devastation before being violently suppressed.",
      "body": "After Poitiers and the king’s capture, rural communities faced plunder, financial demands, and compulsory defensive work. In late spring 1358, insurgents attacked noble castles and in some areas linked their actions with opposition politics in Paris. Guillaume Cale emerged as an important leader, but the movement was not completely unified. In June, forces including those of Charles II of Navarre defeated the rebels, followed by extensive reprisals.",
      "context": "Post-plague changes in labor relations combined with continuing war. Nobles demanded resources while failing to prevent armies and roaming armed companies from attacking the countryside. This challenged the relationship between protection and obedience, though grievances varied from one locality to another.",
      "outcome": "The revolt neither established a lasting government nor comprehensively transformed landholding. Its suppression demonstrated the continuing military capacity of the nobility. Elite accounts often viewed the insurgents with hostility, requiring caution over atrocity stories and casualty totals. The uprising nevertheless reveals rural communities acting collectively in politics.",
      "locationNote": "Beauvais represents the Beauvaisis region. The revolt affected many northern French communities; this marker does not claim to identify its first outbreak.",
