      "date": "May–June 1381",
      "title": "The English rising of 1381",
      "summary": "Poll taxation, labor restrictions, and local conflicts provoked a broad revolt whose participants entered London and confronted the young king.",
      "body": "Resistance in Essex and Kent expanded around tax collection and involved peasants, craftspeople, and other townspeople. Wat Tyler became a prominent leader, while John Ball’s preaching criticized social hierarchy. In June, insurgents entered London, attacked elite property, killed officials, and also committed violence against foreigners. Richard II negotiated and made concessions, but royal control recovered after Tyler was killed during the encounter at Smithfield.",
      "context": "The Black Death reduced population and labor supply. Workers sought higher pay while legislation attempted to constrain wages and mobility. Taxes for war in France added to demands by local lords and church institutions, joining economic grievances to questions of personal freedom.",
      "outcome": "The king revoked many promises and leaders were executed; serfdom did not disappear immediately because of the revolt. The mobilization nevertheless showed ordinary people organizing across regions and making claims about labor, status, and public authority. Its longer significance must be understood alongside gradual social and economic change.",
      "sortOrder": 500
