      "summary": "Milan and Venice made peace at Lodi; a subsequent Italian League temporarily moderated competition among the leading states.",
      "body": "Francesco Sforza, duke of Milan, and Venice ended their war with an agreement at Lodi recognizing their respective positions in Lombardy. Florence supported the settlement, and the papacy and Naples joined the associated alliance arrangements. This did not unite Italy. It attempted to stabilize relations among its leading governments through mutual recognition, diplomacy and collective defence.",
      "context": "Urban wealth, mercenary warfare and dynastic rivalry were closely connected in Italy. A change of dynasty in Milan, Venetian expansion on the mainland and pressure from France and the Ottomans raised the costs of continued conflict and encouraged a negotiated defence of existing interests.",
