      "date": "1513",
      "title": "Machiavelli writes The Prince",
      "summary": "After losing office in Florence, Machiavelli examined power, military organization and political survival in The Prince.",
      "body": "When the Medici returned to Florence, the former republican official Niccolò Machiavelli lost his position and endured imprisonment and torture. During retirement south of the city, he developed his political experience into a short work mentioned in a letter of 1513. The Prince used ancient and contemporary examples to explore how new rulers acquire and retain power, and how armies, popular support, opportunity and personal ability interact.",
      "context": "French and Spanish intervention in the Italian Wars threatened the independence of the peninsula’s states. Machiavelli had observed republics, monarchies and mercenary forces during his diplomatic and military service. These experiences led him to question whether conventional moral instruction adequately explained political success and failure.",
      "outcome": "The work circulated initially in manuscript and was printed only in 1532, after its author’s death. Alongside his writings on republics, it reshaped political debate. Treating it simply as a celebration of cruelty obscures enduring disputes about its arguments concerning rulers, the people and liberty.",
      "locationNote": "Florence represents the political and regional setting of the work; the marker does not identify the rural house where it was written.",
      "sortOrder": 0
