      "date": "1516",
      "title": "Erasmus publishes his Greek New Testament",
      "summary": "Erasmus’s Greek New Testament and revised Latin translation brought humanist textual scholarship into debates about reform.",
      "body": "Working with the Basel printer Johann Froben, Erasmus issued a Greek New Testament accompanied by a revised Latin translation and annotations. He compared manuscripts, explained meanings and challenged readings in the customary Latin text. The first edition had limitations imposed by available manuscripts and the pressure of publication, and Erasmus revised it repeatedly. Its larger importance lay in encouraging the study of Christian scripture through languages and textual criticism.",
      "context": "Renaissance humanists urged scholars to return to ancient texts, while printing extended the reach of their findings. Erasmus hoped education, ethics and scriptural study would renew the church. He did not advocate an immediate break with Rome, although his scholarship entered increasingly divisive religious debates.",
      "outcome": "The edition became an important resource for biblical translators and reformers, including Luther. Erasmus continued to defend moderation, peace and reform within the church, and later disputed free will with Luther. Humanism and Protestant reform consequently remained connected but distinct projects.",
      "sortOrder": 0
