      "date": "31 October 1517",
      "title": "Luther challenges indulgences in the Ninety-five Theses",
      "summary": "Luther’s challenge to indulgences spread through letters, print and controversy, turning a theological dispute into a wider reform movement.",
      "body": "Martin Luther, a theology professor at Wittenberg, prepared ninety-five propositions about indulgences and wrote to Archbishop Albert of Mainz. He challenged assurances that purchasing indulgences secured salvation and called for debate about repentance, papal authority and ecclesiastical wealth. Printed circulation carried the theses far beyond an academic setting. The familiar scene of Luther posting them on the Castle Church door belongs to a historical tradition whose details remain disputed.",
      "context": "Indulgence revenues were connected to rebuilding St Peter’s in Rome and to the financial obligations of senior clergy. Existing calls for church reform, humanist textual scholarship and princely attitudes toward ecclesiastical power shaped the controversy. Luther began with a theological challenge, rather than a complete plan for a European Protestant organization.",
      "outcome": "The dispute widened into questions about faith, scripture and authority. Luther was excommunicated and later examined at Worms; some princes protected him, while reform developed in several directions. The year 1517 is therefore a conventional starting point for changes in institutions and society that unfolded over generations.",
      "sortOrder": 1031
