      "date": "17–18 April 1521",
      "title": "Luther’s hearing at Worms",
      "summary": "Luther’s refusal to retract before the emperor and imperial representatives bound the theological dispute more closely to territorial politics.",
      "body": "On 17 April 1521 Luther appeared under safe conduct at the bishop’s palace in Worms, acknowledged the books presented to him and requested time to consider the demand for retraction. The following day he refused a general recantation, insisting that scripture and clear argument must persuade him rather than authority alone compelling him against conscience. Charles V defended the Catholic faith inherited from his predecessors. The proceedings connected imperial authority, ecclesiastical condemnation and negotiation with territorial rulers. The familiar “Here I stand” wording belongs to a later textual tradition.",
      "context": "Since the indulgence dispute of 1517, Luther had challenged sacramental teaching, papal authority and understandings of salvation. The pope excommunicated him early in 1521. Charles needed to defend Christian unity while bargaining with the imperial estates. Print, urban support and protection from the Saxon elector transformed the treatment of one theologian into a question about the empire’s political order.",
      "outcome": "The subsequent Edict of Worms outlawed Luther and his writings, but enforcement depended on territorial rulers. Saxon protection placed him secretly at the Wartburg, where he worked on a German New Testament and other writings. Reform continued to spread in directions he could not entirely control. Religious division deepened as territorial governments and local communities developed different policies across the empire.",
