      "date": "November 1534",
      "title": "England’s Act of Supremacy",
      "summary": "Parliament recognized Henry VIII as supreme head of the Church of England, giving legal form to the breach with papal authority.",
      "body": "Parliament acknowledged Henry VIII and his successors as the supreme head on earth of the Church of England and empowered the crown to correct ecclesiastical abuses. The act was a key stage in a series of measures restricting appeals to Rome and reorganizing church jurisdiction. Archbishop Thomas Cranmer had already annulled Henry’s marriage to Catherine of Aragon in 1533; the marriage judgment and the restructuring of church jurisdiction thus formed connected but distinct stages.",
      "context": "Henry’s attempt to secure an annulment for dynastic and marital reasons encountered papal politics and canon law. Reforming ideas, royal financial interests and Parliament’s legislative capacity helped turn the dispute into institutional change. Rejecting papal jurisdiction did not entail immediate acceptance of all Protestant doctrine.",
      "outcome": "The crown strengthened its control of the church and subsequently dissolved monasteries, redistributed their property and punished opponents of royal religious authority. Policy changed further under Edward VI, Mary I and Elizabeth I. The settlement of 1534 was a major turning point rather than the final form of English Protestantism.",
