      "date": "1536",
      "title": "The first edition of Calvin’s Institutes",
      "summary": "Calvin published the first Institutes in Basel before moving to Geneva, where he helped organize and spread Reformed Christianity.",
      "body": "The French religious exile John Calvin published the first Latin edition of the Institutes of the Christian Religion in Basel. It offered a compact account of Christian teaching and a defence of persecuted Protestants, prefaced by an appeal to the French king. Repeated expansion later turned this brief book into a major theological work. In the same year, Guillaume Farel persuaded Calvin to remain in Geneva and assist reform there; the place of publication should not be confused with his later centre of activity.",
      "context": "The Reformation after Luther was not a single movement. Reform experiments in Swiss cities, persecution in France and international print networks shaped Calvin’s opportunities. Reformers continued to disagree about communion, church discipline and the relationship between civil and religious authority.",
      "outcome": "The Institutes, together with Geneva’s schools, ministerial training and correspondence networks, helped shape Reformed churches in France, the Netherlands, Britain and beyond. Geneva also enforced moral discipline and punished heresy, making its legacy part of debates about authority and toleration as well as faith and education.",
