      "title": "Copernicus publishes On the Revolutions",
      "summary": "Printed in Nuremberg, On the Revolutions presented a Sun-centred planetary system and opened a lasting astronomical debate.",
      "body": "Johannes Petreius printed Nicolaus Copernicus’s On the Revolutions of the Heavenly Spheres in Nuremberg after Rheticus helped prepare the work for publication. It treated Earth as a planet orbiting the Sun and explained daily celestial motion through Earth’s rotation. An unsigned preface added by Andreas Osiander presented the system as a calculating hypothesis. Copernicus still employed circular motions and elaborate geometrical devices; his model was not the later mechanics of Newton.",
      "context": "European astronomy had long worked within the Ptolemaic tradition and an Earth-centred cosmos. Problems connecting calendars, observations and mathematical models encouraged reconsideration. Humanist textual study and printing created channels through which alternative systems could reach a scholarly audience.",
      "outcome": "The book initially attracted debate mainly among astronomers and did not immediately persuade most readers. Kepler’s orbital research, Galileo’s telescopic observations and Newton’s mechanics subsequently changed the evidence and explanatory framework. Publication in 1543 marked a major stage in a continuing transformation.",
