      "date": "13 December 1545",
      "title": "Council of Trent opens",
      "summary": "Trent combined doctrinal definition with internal reform, creating an enduring framework for Catholic renewal through interrupted sessions.",
      "body": "The council convened by Paul III opened at Trent on 13 December 1545 under papal legates. Bishops and theologians developed proposals in working discussions before formal sessions approved them. Three major periods of activity, separated by long interruptions, continued until 1563. The council treated scripture and tradition, justification, sacraments and discipline together. It emphasized the role of grace in salvation while rejecting the Lutheran formulation of justification by faith alone, and required improvements in clerical education and episcopal residence. It was therefore both a doctrinal response to Protestantism and an arena of reform within Catholicism.",
      "context": "Demands for better clerical conduct, financial administration and pastoral care predated Luther. As the Reformation expanded, Charles V hoped a council would restore imperial unity. Popes worried about conciliar authority and dynastic interference, while France and other monarchies pursued their own strategies. Disagreements over venue, participation and priorities help explain the delays and interruptions.",
      "outcome": "Trent became a central reference for Catholic teaching, priestly training and diocesan government. Seminaries, episcopal visitation and religious orders helped implement its decisions. Implementation required sustained personnel, finance and local coordination and differed with regional conditions. The continuing division of western Christianity and the renewed Catholic institutions helped shape more sharply defined confessional cultures.",
