      "summary": "Imperial law recognized Catholic and Lutheran coexistence through territorial authority, emigration provisions and negotiated exceptions.",
      "body": "Ferdinand negotiated for Charles V with the imperial estates, producing the Religious Peace of Augsburg on 25 September 1555. It protected Catholic estates and adherents of the Augsburg Confession, prohibited religious war between territories and assigned major religious decisions to territorial authorities. Dissenting subjects could sell property and migrate under specified conditions. Some imperial cities retained mixed confessional arrangements, while an ecclesiastical prince who converted was required to surrender office and territory. The later phrase “whose rule, his religion” reflects the leading role of territorial authority, alongside separate provisions for cities and ecclesiastical territories.",
      "context": "The Schmalkaldic War, the Augsburg Interim of 1548 and the princes’ revolt of 1552 demonstrated that imperial military victory could not restore religious unity. Jurisdiction, territorial rights and church property were intertwined, and Catholic rulers also wanted to avoid further conflict. Participants therefore accepted workable coexistence while retaining the hope of eventual religious reconciliation.",
      "outcome": "The peace supplied a legal framework for decades of relative stability while assigning religious decisions primarily to territorial authority and restricting individual choice. Reformed Christians and Anabaptists remained outside its formal protections. The ecclesiastical reservation and local exceptions generated continuing disputes that later interacted with dynastic rivalry and other pressures. Westphalia revised this existing legal structure in 1648 and extended recognition to the Reformed confession.",
