      "summary": "Supremacy and uniformity legislation restored royal ecclesiastical authority and English worship as the basis of a new settlement.",
      "body": "After Elizabeth I’s accession, Parliament passed a new Act of Supremacy and an Act of Uniformity. The queen became supreme governor of the church, royal jurisdiction independent of Rome was restored, and a revised Book of Common Prayer became mandatory. Some traditional ceremonial forms remained, but the institutional direction was Protestant. Enforcement depended on appointments, visitations, oaths and local officials; the population did not simply agree at once.",
      "context": "Repeated changes of religion under Henry VIII, Edward VI and Mary I had disrupted property, office and belief. Elizabeth needed to secure her title and stabilize government while responding both to Catholic opposition and to Protestants who wanted more extensive reform.",
      "outcome": "The measures supplied a durable basis for the Elizabethan church, but Catholic and Puritan dissent persisted. International tensions and rebellion brought stronger enforcement and penalties. The settlement was principally a state-established religious order, not a grant of universal religious freedom.",
