      "title": "The St Bartholomew’s Day Massacre",
      "summary": "Targeted murders of Huguenot leaders in Paris widened into mass killing and spread to other French cities.",
      "body": "The marriage of Henry of Navarre and Margaret of Valois brought many Huguenot nobles to Paris. After Admiral Coligny survived an assassination attempt, members of the royal government decided to eliminate leading Protestants amid escalating fear and tension. Killing began during the night of 23–24 August and expanded into attacks on ordinary Huguenots by armed groups and townspeople. Similar violence followed in provincial cities. Decisions at court, local initiative and collective panic interacted rather than following one perfectly coordinated plan.",
      "context": "France had experienced recurring religious warfare since 1562. The interests of the monarchy, noble families and urban communities were entangled with confessional divisions. A wedding intended to ease the conflict took place in a city shaped by mistrust, court rivalry and organized hostility to Protestantism.",
      "outcome": "Thousands were killed, although the total remains disputed. Survivors converted or fled, Huguenot confidence in royal protection collapsed, and war intensified. The massacres also encouraged arguments about resistance to unjust rulers. Peace emerged only much later through further conflict and negotiation.",
