      "summary": "Telescopic observations of the Moon, Milky Way and Jupiter’s satellites entered European debate through Galileo’s Starry Messenger.",
      "body": "Galileo improved existing telescope technology, made astronomical observations at Padua and published the Starry Messenger in Venice. It described an uneven lunar surface, the numerous stars composing the Milky Way and four bodies orbiting Jupiter. Naming the satellites after the Medici connected the scientific report to court patronage. Later that year he left Padua for service at the Tuscan court.",
      "context": "Copernicus had proposed a moving Earth, but observational evidence and physical explanations remained contested. The new instrument enlarged the visible world while raising questions about optical reliability and reproducibility. Astronomers had to persuade readers that phenomena seen through lenses deserved confidence.",
