      "summary": "William’s invasion and James II’s flight led Parliament to offer the crown to William and Mary and redefine royal authority.",
      "body": "James II’s religious policies, appointments and succession plans provoked opposition. A group of English political figures invited William of Orange to intervene. William landed with a Dutch army in November 1688, and James fled to France as his support collapsed. The Convention Parliament of 1689 declared the throne vacant, offered it jointly to William and Mary and set out rights later embodied in the Bill of Rights.",
      "context": "The crisis combined English fears of Catholic monarchy with William’s European strategy against Louis XIV. The birth of James’s son made a continuing Catholic dynasty possible. Political and religious groups with differing interests therefore temporarily cooperated in support of an external military intervention.",
      "outcome": "The settlement strengthened Parliament’s position in lawmaking, finance and succession, while drawing Britain more deeply into European coalition warfare. Descriptions of a bloodless revolution reflect a limited English perspective: serious wars followed in Scotland and Ireland. Religious toleration remained restricted, and Catholics continued to face institutional exclusion.",
      "sortOrder": 0
