      "summary": "Peter I built a fortress and new city at the Neva’s mouth, linking the Baltic struggle to imperial government and reform.",
      "body": "During the Great Northern War, Russian forces secured the Neva estuary and Peter ordered construction of the Peter and Paul Fortress. A city developed around military, maritime and administrative requirements. It was not created in an entirely empty landscape: settlements and Swedish rule had preceded it. Conscripted labourers, soldiers and artisans carried out arduous construction, while nobles and officials were required to contribute and relocate.",
      "context": "Russia sought a reliable Baltic outlet while transforming its army, navy and administration. Peter’s experience in western Europe shaped some technical and institutional choices. The new city was nevertheless also an outcome of coercive mobilization, wartime finance and monarchical authority, rather than simply cultural imitation.",
      "outcome": "St Petersburg became the principal capital around 1712 and drew in court life, diplomacy and maritime commerce. Sweden recognized Russia’s possession of the region in the peace of 1721. The city displayed the state’s capacity to reorganize its geography while revealing the heavy demands placed on labour, resources and society.",
