      "summary": "A series of settlements redistributed European and overseas interests while keeping the French and Spanish crowns separate.",
      "body": "Britain, France, Spain, the Dutch Republic and other powers concluded separate treaties rather than signing one identical multilateral document. Philip V retained Spain’s throne but renounced claims to the French succession, preventing a union of the crowns. Britain received Gibraltar, Minorca and colonial advantages, together with the asiento privilege to supply enslaved Africans to Spanish America.",
      "context": "The extinction of Spain’s Habsburg line had raised fears that the French Bourbons might combine France with Spain’s extensive possessions. Prolonged war strained finances. British domestic politics and changes in the Austrian succession altered bargaining positions, allowing military and diplomatic interests to converge on a staged compromise.",
      "outcome": "The settlements reshaped territory and commerce, strengthened Britain’s maritime position and restricted a dynastic union of France and Spain. Peace between France and Austria followed in the Rastatt and Baden arrangements of 1714. The war did not end everywhere on one day in 1713, nor did the settlement terminate imperial competition.",
