      "title": "First volume of the Encyclopédie",
      "summary": "Diderot and d’Alembert’s collaborative publication connected scholarship, trades and ideas while navigating sustained censorship and commercial pressures.",
      "body": "The first volume appeared in Paris in 1751 under Diderot and d’Alembert’s principal editorship. Contributors worked alongside booksellers, printers and illustrators. D’Alembert’s Preliminary Discourse and the diagram of knowledge explained its organization, while entries and cross-references connected philosophy, science, arts and mechanical trades. Attention to tools, production and artisans’ experience brought practical knowledge, often accorded lower status, into an ambitious scholarly publication. Its contributors brought differing philosophical, political and religious positions, making the work a space for competing ideas.",
      "context": "The project expanded from plans to translate Chambers’s Cyclopaedia and depended on subscriptions, specialist research and correspondence. Royal censorship, ecclesiastical authority and publishing interests all shaped its development. The costly volumes initially reached readers able to buy them. Price, language and education continued to shape access, while later extracts and adaptations helped ideas circulate more widely.",
      "outcome": "Prohibitions and the withdrawal of privileges in 1752 and 1759 complicated publication, but editors and publishers continued through changing forms of protection and negotiation. The seventeen text volumes were completed in 1765, and eleven plate volumes appeared through 1772. Supplements and adaptations extended its influence. The work spread Enlightenment debate while demonstrating how knowledge production depended on divided labour, commercial finance and relations with political authority.",
