      "title": "The Lisbon earthquake and reconstruction",
      "summary": "Earthquake, tsunami and fire devastated Lisbon, prompting major reconstruction and European debate about catastrophe and reason.",
      "body": "On All Saints’ Day a powerful earthquake struck Lisbon and surrounding areas. Tsunami waves and prolonged fires greatly increased the destruction. The royal government organized relief, restored order and cleared debris. Sebastião José de Carvalho e Melo, later marquis of Pombal, played a central role in rebuilding. The lower city acquired a planned street grid and new building systems, combining administrative authority, commercial needs and construction techniques.",
      "context": "Lisbon was a major port of the Portuguese Empire and Atlantic trade, with densely inhabited districts, warehouses and religious buildings. The catastrophe occurred during an expansion of natural inquiry and Enlightenment debate. Observers compared reports and investigated physical causes while also disputing the relationship between suffering and providence.",
      "outcome": "Deaths and material losses were immense, but casualty totals and seismic magnitude remain estimates derived from different kinds of evidence. Reconstruction created the Pombaline lower town, while writers including Voltaire made the disaster part of European intellectual history. It also tested relief administration and strengthened central authority.",
      "sortOrder": 1101
