      "summary": "Prussia’s invasion of Saxony opened a European war that became linked with British–French imperial conflict across several continents.",
      "body": "Frederick II of Prussia invaded Saxony in 1756, opening full-scale continental war. Austria sought to recover Silesia with support from France, Russia and other allies, while Britain backed Prussia. Repeated campaigns in central Europe connected with conflicts in North America, the Caribbean, Africa and India. Anglo-French fighting in North America had already begun in 1754, so the global conflict did not start everywhere at the same moment.",
      "context": "The War of the Austrian Succession had left territorial disputes unresolved, while Britain and France competed for trade and imperial influence. Diplomatic realignment around 1756 disrupted older alliances. Local campaigns became connected to transcontinental resources, naval forces and commercial networks, involving many governments and local populations.",
      "outcome": "The Treaty of Paris in 1763 redistributed British, French and Spanish overseas interests, while Hubertusburg confirmed Prussian possession of Silesia. Britain’s overseas advantage expanded, but both Britain and France carried heavy debts that encouraged further fiscal conflict. France retained some colonial possessions; it did not permanently lose every overseas territory.",
