      "title": "Catherine II convenes the Legislative Commission",
      "summary": "Catherine’s Enlightenment-inspired Instruction guided discussion of a new law code, but autocracy, privilege and serfdom constrained reform.",
      "body": "Catherine II summoned representatives of territories and legally defined social groups to begin discussing a new code in Moscow. Her Instruction drew on writers including Montesquieu and Beccaria to discuss law, punishment and government while maintaining that Russia required autocratic rule. Delegates voiced numerous local and corporate demands. Privately owned serfs lacked independent representation, placing a fundamental boundary around the reform project.",
      "context": "The empire’s size, inherited laws, local customs and proliferating administrative decrees created problems of legal coherence. Catherine sought useful information and a reputation for enlightened government. Her authority nonetheless depended on the nobility and armed forces, and the language of rational law could not automatically overcome entrenched interests.",
