      "summary": "National Assembly deputies gathered in a Versailles tennis court and pledged to remain united until France had a constitution.",
      "body": "The Third Estate and its allies declared themselves a National Assembly on 17 June. Three days later, finding their usual hall closed, deputies moved to a nearby indoor tennis court. Under Jean-Sylvain Bailly’s presidency, they pledged to continue meeting wherever circumstances required until a constitution had been established. A dispute about voting procedure thereby became an open assertion that national representatives possessed authority of their own.",
