      "title": "Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen",
      "summary": "The constituent assembly made liberty, equality before the law, and national sovereignty foundations of a new political order.",
      "body": "Following August’s decisions against feudal privilege, the National Constituent Assembly adopted a seventeen-article declaration. It named liberty, property, security, and resistance to oppression as natural rights; law was to express the general will, and taxation and public office were to follow common rules. The text challenged privileges based on status and supplied principles for a constitution. Its universal language did not immediately confer equal political citizenship on women or enslaved people in the colonies.",
