      "title": "France abolishes monarchy and becomes a republic",
      "summary": "The National Convention abolished monarchy; republican dating began the next day, marking a new phase of the revolution.",
      "body": "After the Paris insurrection of 10 August, Louis XVI had already been suspended. On 21 September, the newly elected National Convention abolished royalty; on 22 September official acts began to be dated Year One of the French Republic. The change followed Valmy amid foreign war and domestic violence. Convention factions soon fought over the king’s fate, military policy, and social measures: establishing a republic did not unite the revolutionary coalition.",
      "context": "The constitutional monarchy of 1791 had been undermined by the king’s attempted flight, royal dealings with foreign powers, and military setbacks. Radical Parisians increasingly distrusted the crown. The Legislative Assembly’s settlement could not absorb the combined military and social pressures, prompting elections for a new Convention.",
      "outcome": "Louis XVI was executed in January 1793, and war with European monarchies widened. The republic offered broader male political participation while facing factional conflict, provincial revolt, and emergency government. The later revolutionary calendar retrospectively made 22 September 1792 the beginning of its era.",
