      "summary": "War and civil conflict brought expanded emergency powers, political trials, provincial repression, and mass mobilization.",
      "body": "In autumn 1793 the Convention, Committee of Public Safety, and representatives on mission combined wartime mobilization with action against alleged enemies of the revolution. Measures such as the Law of Suspects expanded arrests, revolutionary courts accelerated trials, and price controls and requisitions sought to supply armies and towns. Robespierre was important, but the Terror was not simply one man’s command: civil war, factional struggles, and administrative networks shaped its violence.",
      "context": "Foreign war, rebellions including the Vendée, currency depreciation, and food shortages encouraged emergency measures in the name of revolutionary survival. Conflicting interpretations of popular sovereignty, religious reform, and economic regulation also helped turn political opponents into supposed enemies.",
      "outcome": "Large numbers were imprisoned or executed, including revolutionary participants such as de Gouges. The Thermidorian overthrow of Robespierre and his allies in July 1794 began the contraction of emergency institutions. Violence did not immediately disappear, and the conflict between rights and coercive revolutionary politics remained a lasting problem.",
