      "summary": "Redistributed seats and revised voting qualifications gave industrial towns more representation while retaining property and gender exclusions.",
      "body": "Under pressure from a sustained reform movement and political crisis, Earl Grey’s government changed parliamentary representation in England and Wales. The act removed or reduced seats in depopulated “rotten boroughs,” redistributed seats to growing towns, and revised property qualifications in counties and boroughs. It enlarged the electorate, especially among middle-class men, without creating universal suffrage or ending the political influence of landed wealth.",
