      "body": "Following the Marne, repeated attempts to outflank the enemy carried the fighting north into Belgian Flanders. German forces tried to break through near Ypres, where British and French troops resisted; Indian formations also took part. Attacks and counterattacks around villages such as Gheluvelt caused severe losses. The Allies retained the town and their links towards the Channel ports, but months of combat severely depleted Britain’s pre-war professional army.",
      "context": "The “Race to the Sea” was a succession of attempts to find an open flank, rather than a race for the coast itself. Ypres mattered for its roads and railways, but the salient projecting into enemy territory exposed defenders to fire from several directions.",
      "outcome": "Germany failed to achieve a breakthrough towards the Channel ports. By winter, a front extended from the Channel towards Switzerland and prolonged trench warfare became characteristic of the western campaign. Ypres remained at the centre of a salient devastated by repeated battles.",
      "locationNote": "The Ypres city marker represents the surrounding salient, whose battles involved numerous villages and positions outside the town.",
      "sortOrder": 1019
