      "body": "On 22 April, German troops released chlorine north of Ypres, allowing the wind to carry it into French positions that included North African units. Suffocation, casualties and panic opened a gap. Canadian and other Allied troops organised resistance that prevented a decisive German breakthrough. During the following weeks, repeated gas attacks accompanied fighting around the salient; the defenders shortened their lines but retained Ypres.",
      "context": "In 1915 the combatants searched for ways to break trench deadlock. Irritant chemicals had already been used, so Ypres marks a major beginning of large-scale lethal gas warfare, rather than the first use of any chemical weapon in history. Protective equipment and training were initially inadequate.",
