      "summary": "Strikes and women’s demonstrations in Petrograd develop into military mutiny, abdication and a divided revolutionary authority.",
      "body": "On 8 March, or 23 February in the Julian calendar then used in Russia, women’s demonstrations and workers’ strikes spread through Petrograd. Repression caused bloodshed, after which sections of the garrison refused orders and joined the uprising. Duma politicians formed a provisional committee while workers and soldiers organised a soviet. Nicholas II abdicated at Pskov on 15 March. The following day, his brother Michael declined to accept the throne without confirmation of the people’s will, ending Romanov rule.",
