      "summary": "Germany’s attack brought British and French declarations of war; a Soviet invasion from the east completed Poland’s defeat and partition.",
      "body": "On 1 September 1939, German land and air forces launched a major attack on Poland. Polish troops resisted but faced disadvantages in mobility, air power and strategic deployment. Britain and France declared war on Germany on 3 September without relieving the immediate military pressure. Soviet forces invaded from the east on 17 September. Warsaw capitulated after siege and bombardment late in the month; the government went into exile, while military and civilian resistance continued.",
      "context": "Nazi expansionism, demands over Danzig and transit routes, and the collapse of European security after Munich formed the background. The secret German–Soviet division of spheres of influence enabled the attack and partition. Nazi-staged provocations did not make the invasion a defensive war.",
      "outcome": "German and Soviet occupation divided Polish territory and brought executions, deportations, forced labor and repression. Jews under Nazi rule were progressively segregated and later subjected to systematic mass murder. The invasion turned a regional crisis into a European war, while Poland’s government-in-exile and underground remained active.",
