      "title": "Berlin’s border is sealed and the Wall is built",
      "summary": "East German authorities sealed the border around West Berlin, beginning a barrier system that transformed travel and everyday life.",
      "body": "Early on 13 August East German security forces cut roads and transport links to West Berlin and closed the border with barbed wire. Masonry barriers followed. Commuting, family visits and routes of escape were suddenly interrupted. Over the following decades the authorities expanded the system into walls, patrol roads, lighting and surveillance surrounding West Berlin. It was not a finished concrete wall erected in a single day.",
      "context": "Much of the border between East and West Germany was already closed, leaving Berlin as a major route westward. Migration reflected political dissatisfaction, family ties and economic opportunities. The East German leadership feared both the loss of workers and the weakening of its rule.",
      "outcome": "Border controls severely restricted migration, at the cost of divided families and deaths among people attempting escape. The Wall became a symbol of Cold War division. Its opening in 1989 led to gradual dismantling, while its effects on urban space and public memory persisted.",
      "sortOrder": 813
