      "title": "The Prague Spring and Warsaw Pact invasion",
      "summary": "Dubček’s reforms widened public debate and political freedom, until a Soviet-led invasion interrupted the Prague Spring in August.",
      "body": "After becoming party leader in January, Alexander Dubček and other reformers reduced censorship, encouraged public debate and sought economic change within a socialist system. Journalists, writers and citizens participated actively. Soviet leaders and their allies feared a loss of control. During the night of 20–21 August invading forces entered Czechoslovakia, detained reform leaders and compelled them to accept arrangements that curtailed the reform programme.",
      "context": "Economic difficulties, the legacy of Stalinist repression and relations between Czechs and Slovaks had generated pressure for change. Reformers did not uniformly advocate leaving the socialist bloc, but Soviet leaders regarded political liberalization as a threat that could spread.",
      "outcome": "The subsequent “normalization” restored strict political control and removed many reform supporters from public life. Some critics later turned toward civic rights initiatives, including Charter 77. The experience of 1968 became one important historical background to the political transformation of 1989.",
