      "summary": "Student protests in Paris expanded into a nationwide strike, bringing education, working conditions and authority into public dispute.",
      "body": "Protests at Nanterre and the Sorbonne grew after campus closures, arrests and street confrontations. Violence on the night of 10–11 May widened public sympathy. Workers then struck and occupied factories, advancing their own demands over wages and conditions. Government, unions and employers negotiated, while Charles de Gaulle announced the dissolution of the National Assembly on 30 May to regain the political initiative through elections.",
      "context": "Postwar expansion had produced a much larger student population without equivalent changes in university governance. Economic growth had not removed workers’ dissatisfaction with pay and workplace organization. International antiwar activism and criticism of everyday social hierarchies supplied varied intellectual influences.",
      "outcome": "Strikes gradually ended with concessions including wage increases, and the Gaullists won the June election. The movement therefore did not overthrow the government. Educational and cultural changes followed, although the many subsequent transformations of society cannot all be attributed to this single protest cycle.",
