      "role": "Military and religious figure in the Hundred Years’ War",
      "bio": "Joan came from a rural community in northeastern France and said that divine voices guided her. In 1429 she persuaded Charles’s court to support her actions, participated in the relief of Orléans, and helped press for his coronation at Reims. Religious conviction and dynastic legitimacy reinforced each other alongside the efforts of other commanders and soldiers. Burgundian forces captured her in 1430 and transferred her to English control. An ecclesiastical trial at Rouen led to her execution in 1431; the verdict was annulled in 1456. Both proceedings left extensive testimony about her belief, actions, and relationships, while later religious and French national memory repeatedly reshaped her image.",
