      "bio": "Born in Warsaw, Curie moved to Paris in 1891 and pursued radioactivity research with Pierre Curie. She received the 1903 Nobel Prize in Physics and the 1911 Chemistry prize. During the First World War she organised vehicles, equipment and training for mobile X-ray services, with her daughter Irène also participating. X-rays helped surgeons locate fragments and fractures. Her career shows how scientists, women and medical workers participated in total war, while also revealing radiation hazards that were not yet adequately controlled.",
      "relations": "Pierre Curie: husband and scientific collaborator; Irène Curie: daughter and wartime radiology assistant; Henri Becquerel: fellow recipient of the 1903 Physics prize.",
